+(duration-type)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole
+number of seconds, like `120`, or one or more groups of an integer followed by a unit: `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m`
+(minutes), or `s` (seconds). Each unit may be used at most once, in that order, without spaces. Units are case
+insensitive, so `1h30m`, `1H30M`, and `90m` are all valid. Negative numbers and decimals are not accepted. A
+{class}`~datetime.timedelta` passed as a default or Python argument is returned unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=120'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
